i>Pulte Homes</span><span><i style="background:#e87ba4"></i>Meritage Homes</span><span><i style="background:#c9c1b4"></i>Every other builder</span></div><figcaption>Each dot is a new home a builder sold to its first owner in those 12 months. Map: Chapter3 Realty, from county deeds, county parcel records and Census map lines.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each town has its own leaders. D.R. Horton sold the most in Conway, Loris and Surfside Beach, and Lennar in Longs, Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach:</p><table class="bd-t bd-towns"><caption>New homes builders sold, by mailing town</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Town</th><th scope="col" class="num">New homes sold</th><th scope="col">Builders that sold the most there</th><th scope="col" class="num">Middle price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/conway/">Conway</a></th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">1,478</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">D.R. Horton&nbsp;678, Lennar&nbsp;207, Mungo Homes&nbsp;107</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$312,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Longs</th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">1,340</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">Lennar&nbsp;298, Meritage Homes&nbsp;282, D.R. Horton&nbsp;252</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$328,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/myrtle-beach/">Myrtle Beach</a></th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">1,131</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">Lennar&nbsp;229, D.R. Horton&nbsp;219, Pulte Homes&nbsp;177</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$418,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/north-myrtle-beach/">North Myrtle Beach</a></th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">383</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">Lennar&nbsp;115, Pulte Homes&nbsp;114, Ryan Homes&nbsp;63</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$503,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Loris</th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">315</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">D.R. Horton&nbsp;92, Lennar&nbsp;66, Starlight Homes&nbsp;47</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$276,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/little-river/">Little River</a></th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">256</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">Five community companies&nbsp;64, D.R. Horton&nbsp;62, Ashton Woods&nbsp;59</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$327,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/murrells-inlet/">Murrells Inlet</a></th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">135</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">Ryan Homes&nbsp;50, Toll Brothers&nbsp;37, Lennar&nbsp;17</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$425,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Aynor</th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">125</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">Starlight Homes&nbsp;57, Howell Homes&nbsp;31, Great Southern Homes&nbsp;22</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$305,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="/submarkets/surfside-beach/">Surfside Beach</a></th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">70</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">D.R. Horton&nbsp;47, Ocean Palms Builders&nbsp;13, Ameri Built Homes&nbsp;10</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$284,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Galivants Ferry</th><td class="num" data-l="New homes sold">42</td><td class="txt" data-l="Builders that sold the most">D.R. Horton&nbsp;41</td><td class="num" data-l="Middle price">$272,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="bd-note">Our count of county deeds for the same 12 months, with the number each builder sold in that town. A town here is the mailing address, not city limits. A Myrtle Beach address includes Carolina Forest and Socastee. Towns with 40 or more new homes sold.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where the largest builders sold the most</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">These are the communities each builder sold the most homes in, by the name on its own sales pages:</p><ul class="bd-plain bd-2" aria-label="Where the largest builders sold the most homes"><li><b>D.R. Horton</b>Auberon Woods in Conway and Eden Springs in Longs.</li><li><b>Lennar</b>The Overlook in Longs, which the county records as Indigo Grove, and Kingston Oaks in Conway.</li><li><b>Ryan Homes</b>Grande Dunes North Village, which has Longs mailing addresses.</li><li><b>Pulte Homes</b>Crescent Cove in Myrtle Beach, and more homes in North Myrtle Beach and Longs.</li><li><b>Meritage Homes</b>Cypress Ridge in Longs. Nearly all its homes have Longs addresses.</li><li><b>Mungo Homes</b>Arcadia in Myrtle Beach.</li><li><b>Starlight Homes</b>Spring Grove in Aynor, Andalusia in Loris and Bayview in Conway.</li><li><b>Toll Brothers</b>North Myrtle Beach, Murrells Inlet and Myrtle Beach.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Three communities let you compare builders in one visit. In each, two or more builders each sold 10 or more homes. In Arcadia, they are Mungo Homes, Pulte Homes and Lennar. In Waterbridge, they are D.R. Horton and the community company Waterbridge SC LLC. In SayeBrook, they are Toll Brothers, Pulte Homes and Carolina Cottage Homes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">What the ten busiest new communities have, from pools to boat docks, is in <a href="/buyers/new-construction/#where" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our new construction guide</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Compare two builders in one afternoon</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the town and your price. We pick the builders that build there at that price and set up the visits, as your agent from the first one.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us plan your builder visits</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What they cost">What do new homes from each builder cost?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Among the 16, the middle sale price ran from about $258,000 for Lennar and $259,000 for Starlight Homes to about $612,000 for Toll Brothers. The table gives the price range of each builder's sales, and what it asks for the homes it has listed now:</p><table class="bd-t bd-prices"><caption>What each builder's new homes sold for, and what it asks now</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Builder</th><th scope="col" class="num">Sold for, middle half</th><th scope="col" class="num">Middle asking price now</th><th scope="col" class="num">Listed now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">D.R. Horton</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$279,000 to $375,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$328,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">248</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lennar</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$232,000 to $308,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$249,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">60</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ryan Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$309,000 to $523,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">&mdash;</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Pulte Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$419,000 to $623,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$565,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">31</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meritage Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$300,000 to $382,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$320,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">29</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Five community companies</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$410,000 to $619,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">&mdash;</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">&mdash;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mungo Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$325,000 to $492,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$402,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">60</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Great Southern Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$300,000 to $350,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$332,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">62</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Beverly Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$300,000 to $394,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$340,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">60</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Beazer Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$338,000 to $500,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$380,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">40</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dream Finders Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$273,000 to $500,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$620,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">32</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Starlight Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$230,000 to $288,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$272,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">18</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Toll Brothers</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$503,000 to $692,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">&mdash;</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ashton Woods</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$255,000 to $450,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$487,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">15</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">DRB Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$291,000 to $378,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">$310,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">27</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Howell Homes</th><td class="num" data-l="Sold for">$273,000 to $327,000</td><td class="num" data-l="Asking now">&mdash;</td><td class="num" data-l="Listed now">&mdash;</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="bd-note">Sold: our count of county deeds for the same 12 months. Half of each builder's homes sold inside the range shown. Asking: new homes for sale in the MLS on the day we counted. Under 15 listed, the count only. A dash means we could not match the builder's listings to its deeds. Great Southern Homes' listings are Stanley Martin's.</p><div class="bd-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1,090 new homes were listed for sale in Horry County on the day we counted, counting townhouses and condos. 280 of them asked $300,000 or less.</p><div class="bd-stat"><p class="k">On a budget of $300,000</p><ul><li><strong>280</strong><span>new homes listed at $300,000 or less</span></li><li><strong>83</strong><span>of them by D.R. Horton, the most of any builder</span></li><li><strong>45</strong><span>by Lennar, the next most</span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At that price, Beverly Homes listed 22, Stanley Martin and Starlight Homes 16 each, and DRB Homes 13. Every one is on <a href="/homes/map/?has=new_construction,county_horry&amp;max=300000" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our map of new homes for sale</a>.</p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why some builders are hard to find in the listings</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many builders' homes never reach the MLS, the database brokers share to list homes for sale. A listing search shows only part of what is being built.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ryan Homes sold 477 homes here in those 12 months and had 1 listed in the MLS that day. Lennar sold 967 and had 60 listed. We ask those builders for their homes for sale directly.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Lot premiums, options and the first full tax bill come on top of these prices. Our guide works through <a href="/buyers/new-construction/#tax-bill" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what the price leaves out</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="owners"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who owns which name">Who owns the builder names on signs and deeds?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Several builders here sell under one name and sign deeds under another, and two have been bought by other builders. The name on the deed is the company that sold you the house:</p><ul class="bd-plain bd-2" aria-label="Who owns the builder names on signs and deeds"><li><b>Ryan Homes</b>A trade name of NVR, Inc., so NVR is the seller on county deeds for Ryan Homes. NVR builds Ryan Homes mainly for first-time buyers and buyers moving up from their first home.</li><li><b>Mungo Homes</b>Part of Clayton Properties Group, a Berkshire Hathaway company, since December 2018. Clayton Properties Group is the seller on its deeds here.</li><li><b>Great Southern Homes</b>A South Carolina builder. It became part of Stanley Martin Homes on May 4, 2026, when Stanley Martin bought its parent company. Its homes for sale here are now listed by Stanley Martin's sales company.</li><li><b>Starlight Homes</b>An Ashton Woods company. Ashton Woods also sells homes here under its own name.</li><li><b>Dream Finders Homes</b>It came to Myrtle Beach by buying the homebuilding business of H&amp;H Constructors of Fayetteville. We count the deeds of that company under Dream Finders.</li><li><b>Beazer Homes</b>Part of Dream Finders Homes since October 20, 2026. Its homes on the chart were sold before that, under its own name.</li><li><b>Five community companies</b>Banbury SC, Baytree SC, Waterbridge SC, Heritage SC and Cedar Branch SC LLC, each named for a community, sold 239 homes. Each is the only seller named on its deeds.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the seller's name on the contract with you before you sign, so you know which company stands behind the warranty.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Know who you are buying from</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the builder's contract before you sign. We read it with you, line by line, starting with the name of the seller.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us the builder's contract</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="warranty"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Warranty and lender">How do the builders' warranties and lenders compare?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of the large builders state a warranty of 1 year on most of the house and up to 10 years on the structure. Most also own a mortgage company, and at five of them about 8 in 10 buyers used it, each counted the company's own way.</p><table class="bd-t bd-warranty"><caption>The warranty and the mortgage company each builder states in its annual report</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Builder</th><th scope="col">Warranty it states</th><th scope="col">Mortgage company it owns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">D.R. Horton</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">1 year on most of the home, 2 years on major mechanical systems, 10 years on major structural parts</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">DHI Mortgage, which financed 81% of the homes D.R. Horton closed</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lennar</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">At least 1 year on materials and workmanship</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">Yes. It made loans to 84% of Lennar buyers who used a mortgage, where it offered the service</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ryan Homes (NVR)</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">Not stated in its annual report</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">NVR Mortgage, with a capture rate of 86%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Pulte Homes</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">1 year on the whole home, and some parts for up to 10 years</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">Pulte Mortgage, which financed 84.7% of Pulte buyers who used a mortgage</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meritage Homes</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">Generally 1 year on workmanship, 2 years on mechanical systems and up to 10 years on the structure</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">A mortgage joint venture and preferred lenders. It may pay part of the closing costs for buyers who use its preferred lenders</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Toll Brothers</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">A limited 10-year structural warranty for many buyers</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">Toll Brothers Mortgage Company, which financed about 43% of Toll's home closings</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dream Finders Homes</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">1 year on workmanship and materials, and up to 10 years on structural systems</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">Jet HomeLoans, with a capture rate of 79%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Beazer Homes, before its sale</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">1 to 2 years on workmanship and materials, and up to 10 years on certain structural failures</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">None of its own. It named several lenders for each community</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Great Southern Homes, before its sale</th><td class="txt" data-l="Warranty it states">1 year on materials and workmanship, 2 years on plumbing, electrical and heating and air, and 10 years on the structure</td><td class="txt" data-l="Mortgage company it owns">&mdash;</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="bd-note">From each company's annual report for its 2025 fiscal year, nationwide, not Myrtle Beach only. Each company counts its share its own way. A capture rate is the share of buyers who used the mortgage company the builder owns. Beazer and Great Southern Homes: what their reports said before each was sold. A dash means we have no verified figure.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a builder not in the table, we get the warranty booklet from the builder before you sign. Our new construction guide covers <a href="/buyers/new-construction/#warranty-law" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what South Carolina law adds to a warranty</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="ask"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Before you sign">What should you ask a builder before you sign?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six things. We check each one with you before you sign a builder's contract:</p><ol class="bd-check" aria-label="What we check with any builder before you sign"><li><b>The license</b>South Carolina requires a state license for anyone who builds a home for someone else on a job over $5,000. It can be a residential builder license or a general contractor license. Building without one is a misdemeanor. We check the builder's license with the state before you sign.</li><li><b>The warranty, in writing</b>We get the builder's warranty booklet before you sign. It says what each part of the house is covered for, for how long, and who you call.</li><li><b>The HOA</b>We read the homeowners association (HOA) papers with you. They give the monthly fee, what it pays for, and <a href="/hoa/developer-control-turnover/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">when the builder hands the HOA to the owners</a>.</li><li><b>The lot premium and the options</b>A lot premium is an extra charge for a particular lot. We compare it, and the options you pick, with the base price. Our guide lists <a href="/buyers/new-construction/#tax-bill" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what else the price leaves out</a>, including the first full tax bill.</li><li><b>The incentive and its lender</b>Some incentives come only with the builder's own lender. We ask the builder in writing what it offers, and our guide covers <a href="/buyers/new-construction/#incentives" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">whether to use the builder's lender</a>.</li><li><b>Your own agent, from the first visit</b>Tell us before your first visit or online sign-up. We register with the builder as your agent. Our guide explains <a href="/buyers/new-construction/#your-agent" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">why the first visit matters</a>.</li></ol></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="new-home-builders-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">New home builders FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Which home builder in Myrtle Beach is best?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We rank builders by homes sold, not by quality. D.R. Horton sold the most new homes in Horry County in the 12 months we counted (1,397). You can compare where each builds, what its homes cost, and the warranty and lender it states. We read any builder's contract with you before you sign.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is D.R. Horton or Lennar bigger in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">D.R. Horton. It sold 1,397 new homes in Horry County in those 12 months, against 967 for Lennar. Lennar's homes sold for less in the middle, about $258,000 against about $325,000.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why can't I find Ryan Homes in the listings?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Few of its homes are listed in the MLS, the database brokers share. Ryan Homes sold 477 homes here in those 12 months and had 1 listed on the day we counted. We ask the builder for its homes for sale directly.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who is Clayton Properties Group?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Berkshire Hathaway company that owns Mungo Homes. It is the seller on the deeds for Mungo's homes in Horry County.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does a home builder need a license in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, for a job over $5,000. It can be a residential builder license or a general contractor license. Building without one is a misdemeanor.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/882184/000088218425000081/dhi-20250930.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">D.R. Horton 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/920760/000162828026003870/len-20251130.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lennar 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/906163/000090616326000018/nvr-20251231.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NVR 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/822416/000082241626000007/phm-20251231.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PulteGroup 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/833079/000083307926000010/mth-20251231.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Meritage 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/794170/000079417025000112/tol-20251031.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Toll Brothers 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1825088/000162828026010837/dfh-20251231.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dream Finders 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/915840/000091584025000075/bzh-20250930.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Beazer 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/915840/000110465926117198/tm2622398d20_8k.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Beazer 8-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1830188/000183018826000009/uhg-20251231.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UHG 10-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1830188/000110465926054519/tm2613353d1_8k.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UHG 8-K</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1067983/000119312526083899/brka-20251231.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Berkshire 10-K</a>, <a href="https://claytonhomes.com/newsroom/press-releases/Clayton-Properties-Group-Acquires-Mungo-Homes" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Clayton release</a>, <a href="https://www.starlighthomes.com/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Starlight Homes</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&chapter=59" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-59</a>. Builder sales: our count of county deeds, November 2025 to October 2026. Listings: our count, November 8, 2026, in aggregate. Source: Coastal Carolinas Association of REALTORS® Multiple Listing Service (CCMLS).</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We rank builders by homes sold in county records and rate no builder. Nothing on this page is legal advice. For the terms of one builder's contract, talk to a South Carolina attorney.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Part of Auberon Woods in Conway from above. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The National Map (USGS)</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Buy from any builder with your own agent</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">We come to your first visit, read the builder's contract with you, and set up your inspections before you close.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us join your first builder visit</a></div></section></main>
